  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 911 PM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nHigh pressure and ample moisture remain in place which means the\nrunning forecast remains largely the same...afternoon and\nevening convection. Slow storm motions remain in the forecast\nso, as we've seen over the last several days, the stronger \nshowers and storms will cause plenty of heavy rain. Those areas\nsusceptible to flooding will remain under the gun as will burn\nscars like the Babylon, Ferris and Gold Mountain burn scars.\nOutside of the rain, gusty outflow winds will also be possible\nwith gusts of 40 to 50 mph not out of the question. To round it\nout, lightning will be common with some small hail also\npossible. The CAMs are hinting at a slight downturn in\nconvection tomorrow and Tuesday but not sold on those solutions,\nhowever, as CAPE, instability and available moisture remains \nhigh across the CWA.\n\nHighs remain anywhere from 5 to 10 degrees above climatological\nnorms with 100s still in the forecast for many lower elevations.\nOvernight lows will drop, obviously, but still remain above \nnormal and without much relief, the going Heat Advisory remains \nin effect for the Grand Valley and Lower Yampa River Basin \nthrough Tuesday morning. \n\nLooking ahead, a shortwave is progged to move across the area\nWednesday and if this should occur, some stronger thunderstorms\nwill be possible with large hail and/or strong outflow winds.\nKeep an eye on the latest forecast. After that, high pressure\nmay build in to our west. Flow around the high will cause\nmoisture to slowly decrease which will temper convection across\nthe region.",
